{"kind":"report","text":"Planner decision for bba6c6b8 (M1 preparation: the four contract questions), after reading notes/implementation/2026-10-18-trusted-packages-preparation.md at 90e7cef0 in full.\n\nThe split into a developer half and an install half is as the request intended. One gate and one exact-head review per submitted head; the planner pushes after each landing and makes the one host deployment after the install half's review.\n\n1. Historical Counter support. Confirmed as you recommend. \"Gone from the host's source\" means the Counter and lending routes in the host's dispatch, their compiled asset modules, and the application-specific cohort selection at room setup. The historical catalog pins (register6, directory6, membership5) and their judging and replay support stay, because judging and replay must not change and existing histories must still replay. No new room is created with that cohort. The implementation note says this boundary in one paragraph.\n\n2. One install and default grants. Confirmed. One admin-signed install act, recorded by a new version of the room's rules scope, with explicit pending, active and refused state and checked deliveries for activation, the role grants and application creation. It is one member act, not a transaction across scopes. Existing grants remain. Only the default grants the admin names in the install act are added, so the record shows what was requested and what was confirmed. A failed subordinate step stays visible and resumable, and no second signature is invented. This is the applications note's gap 2 (the install act extends activate) and decision 2 (install is per room).\n\n3. URI, trust and the signed release. Confirmed in substance: a Git release resolved by URI (applications note, decision 3), naming an exact immutable commit and the path inside its tree, with the expected release id beside the URI; an operator trust list of exact release id and publisher key; a canonical manifest signed with the publisher's existing Ed25519 key; the release digest covers the signed envelope and the id is not a field inside what it digests. No public registry and no publish service in M1. The URI spelling becomes persistent bytes only in the install half, so propose the exact spelling in the developer half's implementation note, beside pack's output, following the scope-URI direction (request 9626787f: a URI must resolve), and I confirm it before any install entry carries it.\n\n4. Host store and generic route. Confirmed: one private host-owned content store keyed by content id, with strict per-package and per-asset limits, and a route bound to the room, the installation and the exact presentation id. Neither the route nor a same-origin policy is an isolation boundary; only operator-listed trusted packages enter, and the implementation note says M1 does not show that an arbitrary package is safe. The storage binding and the exact route shape are yours within that contract; state them in the install half's note. The deployment is one, after review of that head.\n\nThe evidence section of your note stands as the acceptance for both halves. G0 uses today's scenario runner until dap check lands, as its request says.","body":{"request":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:bba6c6b8c402aa4d9910a49aa8ab9a0ebae48b68","to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}